00:00:08.349 --> 00:00:09.890
JENS KUEHLER: Welcome
to the session.

00:00:09.890 --> 00:00:11.720
I hope you enjoyed
the day so far.

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:12.580
My name is Jens.

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:14.650
I'm a solution
architect at Google.

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:16.770
And I have with me
Raul from BT, who

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:18.940
is a head of [INAUDIBLE]
specialist there,

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:21.330
and who will talk
about that company

00:00:21.330 --> 00:00:23.411
later and our partnership.

00:00:23.411 --> 00:00:25.160
Today I'm going to
talk about Google Cloud

00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:27.310
networking for enterprises.

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:30.820
Now, when you think about
enterprise and the cloud,

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:32.900
there's a few things which
are always important.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:35.520
And I'm going to touch
on quite a few of them,

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:37.960
and how, especially
the networking part

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:40.490
of Google's cloud can
help you with them.

00:00:40.490 --> 00:00:43.240
One of them is scaling
your application.

00:00:43.240 --> 00:00:44.720
That's very important.

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:47.210
That's why you come to the
cloud in the first place.

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:50.580
One is how you keep
all of that secure.

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:52.810
Then always, when you
talk to enterprises,

00:00:52.810 --> 00:00:54.510
you don't move
everything at once.

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:57.520
So you want to
securely connect both

00:00:57.520 --> 00:01:00.320
the old and the new environment.

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:01.730
And then, as a
last thing, I will

00:01:01.730 --> 00:01:04.480
talk about virtual private
cloud, which is a concept which

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:06.680
came over the last few years.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:09.260
And how we are simplifying
it a little bit.

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:12.274
And after that, Raul will talk
about hybrid infrastructure,

00:01:12.274 --> 00:01:13.690
hybrid cloud
infrastructure, which

00:01:13.690 --> 00:01:17.020
is also on top of
[INAUDIBLE] enterprise.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:18.814
But let's start with scale.

00:01:18.814 --> 00:01:20.230
Let's say you build
an application

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:22.610
in the cloud, a
multi-tier application,

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:25.210
there's a few things you
generally care about.

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:29.210
The first is it needs to be
globally available everywhere

00:01:29.210 --> 00:01:32.680
for users around the
globe at a low latency.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:35.860
It needs to be reliable,
always available.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:39.400
You want to order scale with
your demands up and down.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:42.680
And you want to optimize
it for cost as well.

00:01:42.680 --> 00:01:44.710
And then, the best,
if it's portable.

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:47.610
So in case you're not happy,
you can move to another cloud,

00:01:47.610 --> 00:01:50.890
or you can even on premise.

00:01:50.890 --> 00:01:52.900
And how we do that in
Google Cloud Platform

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:55.310
is with some of those
products listed here.

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:59.710
A Cloud Load Balancing, our
CDN, I will talk about now.

00:01:59.710 --> 00:02:02.470
Our interconnect I will
talk in the extra part.

00:02:02.470 --> 00:02:05.350
And then you need some kind
of Google Cloud product

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:06.820
to actually run the application.

00:02:06.820 --> 00:02:08.770
This can be anything,
can be contained

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:11.290
an engine with Kubernetes
to be super portable.

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:14.470
But it can also be
just virtual machines,

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:17.950
managed instance groups,
any of those products.

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:20.080
Let's start with global.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:23.309
You want to have users around
the globe for your application.

00:02:23.309 --> 00:02:25.600
And that's where Google Cloud
Platform with the network

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:28.410
can help you, because we
have a global infrastructure.

00:02:28.410 --> 00:02:30.204
Here you see green dots.

00:02:30.204 --> 00:02:31.620
These are the
regions we currently

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:34.870
are found our platform where
you can run virtual machines.

00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:38.440
And we have blue dots, which are
regions which we will open over

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:41.890
the course of 2017 and 2018.

00:02:41.890 --> 00:02:44.450
But what's really
important is the gray dots

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:45.280
and the blue lines.

00:02:45.280 --> 00:02:46.930
Because that's our
point of presence

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:49.540
which are interconnected
via our network.

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:54.040
And your users will always enter
the network at a close point--

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:57.220
closest point to their
location, regardless of where

00:02:57.220 --> 00:02:58.990
the data is sitting.

00:02:58.990 --> 00:03:01.840
And we have over 100 of
them around the globe.

00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:05.275
And over 80 of them run what
we call the Google Front

00:03:05.275 --> 00:03:08.240
End, which is where
load balancing happens.

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:11.050
So, when you want to load
balance you applications,

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:13.570
we have five flavors
of load balancing.

00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:16.600
But today I want to talk
about global load balancing.

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:22.420
There, we have HTTP, HTTPS load
balancing for HTTP workloads.

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:25.540
For other secure workloads,
we have an SSL proxy.

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:31.930
And then we have a TCP proxy
for unencrypted traffic.

00:03:31.930 --> 00:03:35.140
And all of those use what I just
called out, the Google Front

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:38.050
End, which we have
in over 80 locations.

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:39.790
Now lets look at this in detail.

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:43.110
On the left, you see a
traditional load balancer here.

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:45.250
And on the right,
you see how this

00:03:45.250 --> 00:03:46.630
works on Google Cloud Platform.

00:03:46.630 --> 00:03:48.790
You don't really see
a load balance at all.

00:03:48.790 --> 00:03:50.650
You have these Google
Front Ends sitting

00:03:50.650 --> 00:03:53.090
at the edge of our
network around the globe.

00:03:53.090 --> 00:03:55.510
And they all sync with each
other over global control

00:03:55.510 --> 00:03:57.670
plane, and talk to
each other and know

00:03:57.670 --> 00:03:59.200
where there's
available capacities

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:01.090
to serve your application.

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:04.510
So they can direct your users
to the closest available data

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:05.920
center.

00:04:05.920 --> 00:04:10.030
Now, that means that
users in California

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:12.870
can access the service you
have deployed in the US.

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:15.760
Users in London get
routed automatically

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:18.010
to your VMs in Europe.

00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:20.470
And users in Asia
get automatically

00:04:20.470 --> 00:04:22.580
routed to VMs there.

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:24.580
What's different from
other clouds as well is we

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:27.310
don't use DNS for
load balancing this.

00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:32.500
You get one single IP around
the globe for all of this.

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:35.090
And you can have this
now on IPv4 and now

00:04:35.090 --> 00:04:36.990
as well on IPv6 and better.

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:42.740
Now once you have your
application and your load

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:45.530
balancing set up, you might
want to optimize more for cost

00:04:45.530 --> 00:04:48.230
or even more for latency.

00:04:48.230 --> 00:04:52.180
And caching and CDN makes
absolute sense for that.

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:54.980
In Google Cloud, we have
two options for that.

00:04:54.980 --> 00:04:56.690
You can use Google Cloud CDN.

00:04:56.690 --> 00:04:58.800
You can activate that
with a single click.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:01.120
It's integrated with
our load balancer.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:04.090
Or if you already have an
existing contract with a CDN

00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:06.410
provider and need some
advanced features,

00:05:06.410 --> 00:05:09.800
you can use any of our
partner CDN providers,

00:05:09.800 --> 00:05:13.370
and we give you a discounted
rate for egress to those.

00:05:16.219 --> 00:05:17.760
Now you have the
application running.

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:18.670
It's load balanced.

00:05:18.670 --> 00:05:19.680
It's caching.

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:21.510
But a multi-tiered
application, you

00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:25.770
need to talk to services within
your network with each other.

00:05:25.770 --> 00:05:28.900
And you don't want to
expose those via public IP.

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:32.340
So you have the same
load balancing scale.

00:05:32.340 --> 00:05:35.890
You have externally as well
by an internal load balancer.

00:05:35.890 --> 00:05:39.510
To run this, we use a
technology called Andromeda.

00:05:39.510 --> 00:05:40.820
Now what is Andromeda?

00:05:40.820 --> 00:05:43.170
It's our software defined
networking infrastructure

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:46.290
we built especially for
Google Computer Engine.

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:49.080
And that allows us for
faster startup times,

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:51.420
for high speed and low latency.

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:54.680
And we can quickly
include and augment

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:56.910
our network
virtualization functions,

00:05:56.910 --> 00:05:58.460
such as firewall
I'm talking about

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:02.160
later, on that internal
load balancing.

00:06:02.160 --> 00:06:03.900
We ran through
several iterations

00:06:03.900 --> 00:06:08.010
of the Andromeda data plane
in the last few years.

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:11.580
Currently, we're rolling out
Andromeda 2.1, which helps

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:13.720
you bypass the hypervisor.

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:16.460
So you can talk directly
to the network interface

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:19.590
cards on the machine
in the user space.

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:21.580
And we want to bypass
that directly so you

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:23.850
can talk directly to
the physical hardware

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:26.580
from your virtual machine
in a future iteration.

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:28.320
Now what does that mean for you?

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:30.660
This makes it possible
for us to increase

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:32.700
the speed you get between VMs.

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:35.520
You can now get up to
16 gigabit per seconds.

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:37.560
It leads to lower latency.

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:39.790
But one thing which
is often overlooked

00:06:39.790 --> 00:06:44.320
is it also leads to less CPU
consumption, on the host, where

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:48.000
we run the VM, and on
your VM guest machine.

00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:51.600
And that means, if you
have network and CPU

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:55.110
intensive applications, they run
much more smoothly than before.

00:06:57.780 --> 00:07:00.837
Now this is how we can help
you scale the application.

00:07:00.837 --> 00:07:01.920
Let's talk about security.

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:06.360
Because this is really on
top of mind for enterprises.

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:09.090
In a traditional environment
when you think about security,

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:12.360
you think about routers,
firewalls, DDoS protections,

00:07:12.360 --> 00:07:17.790
and a lot of boxes like IDS,
IPS, WAF application firewalls.

00:07:17.790 --> 00:07:20.760
Lots of this is virtualized
in the Google Cloud.

00:07:20.760 --> 00:07:24.150
So let's look at them, and how
you can move those concepts

00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:26.010
over to the Google Cloud.

00:07:26.010 --> 00:07:27.870
The first thing is
routing and switching.

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:29.700
In a traditional
environment, traffic

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:32.640
comes from the internet, and
moves through several layers

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:35.340
until it gets to the top of
rack switch, and in the end,

00:07:35.340 --> 00:07:38.310
to your physical host.

00:07:38.310 --> 00:07:40.480
In the Google
Cloud Platform, you

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:42.230
have machines talking
to each other.

00:07:42.230 --> 00:07:43.960
But the network
infrastructure in between

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:45.310
is completely virtualized.

00:07:45.310 --> 00:07:47.830
It's run through our
software defined networking.

00:07:47.830 --> 00:07:50.491
So you don't have to
configure anything there.

00:07:50.491 --> 00:07:52.990
And you don't have to care where
the machines are physically

00:07:52.990 --> 00:07:54.860
located at all.

00:07:54.860 --> 00:07:57.220
But if you want to do some
advanced functionality,

00:07:57.220 --> 00:07:58.840
advanced routing.

00:07:58.840 --> 00:08:00.910
For example, you want
to push some traffic

00:08:00.910 --> 00:08:04.150
to a certain address
range through a gateway,

00:08:04.150 --> 00:08:06.610
you can do that with
advanced routing range

00:08:06.610 --> 00:08:08.740
rules as well, virtualized.

00:08:08.740 --> 00:08:13.030
We all of-- we put these
things in place seamlessly

00:08:13.030 --> 00:08:16.820
through our software
defined network.

00:08:16.820 --> 00:08:20.810
In a typical data
center network,

00:08:20.810 --> 00:08:22.850
firewalls are often
a choke point.

00:08:22.850 --> 00:08:26.240
Because firewall capacity
is not the same capacity

00:08:26.240 --> 00:08:28.230
as the rest of the network.

00:08:28.230 --> 00:08:31.700
Now, if you think back
on the previous slide,

00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:33.470
there was no firewall
at all listed

00:08:33.470 --> 00:08:35.030
in the Google Cloud Platform.

00:08:35.030 --> 00:08:37.740
That's because it's distributed.

00:08:37.740 --> 00:08:39.570
And it's on every single host.

00:08:39.570 --> 00:08:43.640
We distribute the firewall, and
it's enforced at a host level.

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:46.515
Now in the past, you had
firewalls on Google Cloud

00:08:46.515 --> 00:08:55.110
Platform so you could save which
traffic can come into a VM.

00:08:55.110 --> 00:08:57.240
But we recently
added new feature.

00:08:57.240 --> 00:09:00.180
One is, you can now
control traffic outgoing

00:09:00.180 --> 00:09:02.520
from a VM egress firewalls.

00:09:02.520 --> 00:09:06.370
And as well, you can set
permit and deny rules.

00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:09.960
So you can have rules
overlapping each other,

00:09:09.960 --> 00:09:14.100
and set those with a priority,
which would get then looked

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:15.370
at in sequence.

00:09:15.370 --> 00:09:19.660
So you can do the same kind of
complex access control lists

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:21.400
you used to form on premise.

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:24.290
You can now move those
more or less one to one

00:09:24.290 --> 00:09:25.505
to the Google Cloud Platform.

00:09:28.590 --> 00:09:32.400
If you think about distributed
denial of service attacks,

00:09:32.400 --> 00:09:35.680
you will be happy to hear that
if you either use Google Cloud

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:37.770
load balancer or
Google Cloud CDN,

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:40.830
we have a built in layer
three, layer four DDoS defense

00:09:40.830 --> 00:09:41.910
already.

00:09:41.910 --> 00:09:44.430
And our global high speed
and high capacity network

00:09:44.430 --> 00:09:46.470
makes sure that we
can absorb attacks

00:09:46.470 --> 00:09:48.990
of any kind of size and scale.

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:51.880
Now, if that's not
enough for you,

00:09:51.880 --> 00:09:53.490
and if you want
additional protection,

00:09:53.490 --> 00:09:56.550
you can use any of our third
party DDoS protection partners,

00:09:56.550 --> 00:09:59.280
and put an additional
layer of defense in.

00:10:02.870 --> 00:10:06.300
Now when you have
all this in place,

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:10.230
you might still want some
specialized security appliance,

00:10:10.230 --> 00:10:14.085
or you're already tied
very closely to a vendor.

00:10:14.085 --> 00:10:16.460
Let's say, for example, you
want to put a web application

00:10:16.460 --> 00:10:18.050
firewall in place.

00:10:18.050 --> 00:10:21.355
Or you're used to running
Checkpoint firewall,

00:10:21.355 --> 00:10:23.480
and you want to use the
same rules in the cloud you

00:10:23.480 --> 00:10:24.980
use on premise.

00:10:24.980 --> 00:10:27.740
Now I'm happy to announce that
most of the major appliance

00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:31.820
vendors now offer a virtualized
version of their appliances,

00:10:31.820 --> 00:10:34.850
which you can launch easily
with a few clicks in our Google

00:10:34.850 --> 00:10:35.755
Cloud Cloud Launcher.

00:10:38.300 --> 00:10:41.400
So to recap, security
is a layered approach.

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:43.820
And this is the
same way we see it.

00:10:43.820 --> 00:10:46.800
At the core, you have a
secure, virtual private cloud.

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:48.250
You have distributed firewall.

00:10:48.250 --> 00:10:50.890
You have identity and
access management.

00:10:50.890 --> 00:10:52.460
And a lot of other
features, some

00:10:52.460 --> 00:10:55.580
of which I will touch
on a little bit later.

00:10:55.580 --> 00:10:58.550
Then, around this, you have
a secure interconnection,

00:10:58.550 --> 00:11:01.260
which I will now go into.

00:11:01.260 --> 00:11:03.140
You can have third party
virtual appliances,

00:11:03.140 --> 00:11:06.470
adding even extra
features to that.

00:11:06.470 --> 00:11:08.690
And then you have
our load balancer,

00:11:08.690 --> 00:11:13.130
which has practically
unlimited capacity for you,

00:11:13.130 --> 00:11:17.270
and has built in layer three,
layer four DDoS protection.

00:11:17.270 --> 00:11:20.940
Around all that, we have
our global Google network,

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:24.800
which can absorb any kind,
style, and kind of attack.

00:11:24.800 --> 00:11:28.790
And then, as an outer layer
of defense, you can use this--

00:11:28.790 --> 00:11:32.390
another third party DDoS defense
mechanism on top of that,

00:11:32.390 --> 00:11:35.380
if you wish.

00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:36.840
Now we talked about scale.

00:11:36.840 --> 00:11:38.580
We talked about security.

00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:40.260
But you also want
to know how you

00:11:40.260 --> 00:11:44.220
want to interconnect your
own existing environment

00:11:44.220 --> 00:11:45.672
with the Google Cloud.

00:11:45.672 --> 00:11:47.880
And I'm happy to tell you
that this got a lot easier.

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:54.060
We used to have only
these three mechanism.

00:11:54.060 --> 00:11:56.070
We have a direct
peering mechanism,

00:11:56.070 --> 00:12:00.150
where you meet us in one
of our points of presence,

00:12:00.150 --> 00:12:01.500
around the globe.

00:12:01.500 --> 00:12:03.960
You have a carrier
interconnect functionality,

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:05.790
where you use a
partner such as BT

00:12:05.790 --> 00:12:07.860
to provide a service for you.

00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:12.960
But all of us only provided a
public view into our products,

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:15.330
like over public IP addresses.

00:12:15.330 --> 00:12:19.110
And the only way you had
to connect privately to us

00:12:19.110 --> 00:12:21.210
was via VPN tunnels.

00:12:24.370 --> 00:12:27.850
The advantage we have, this
private interconnect service

00:12:27.850 --> 00:12:30.230
actually gives you access
to all Google services.

00:12:30.230 --> 00:12:32.890
So not only Google
Cloud Platform, but also

00:12:32.890 --> 00:12:36.220
all G-suite services, like
Docs and Sheets and Mail.

00:12:36.220 --> 00:12:39.380
But even our consumer services,
as YouTube, et cetera,

00:12:39.380 --> 00:12:43.000
are available over that link.

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:46.860
But for private access,
you will limit it to VPN.

00:12:46.860 --> 00:12:48.870
We do have a high
availability option

00:12:48.870 --> 00:12:51.090
there. so you can have
one or multiple tunnels.

00:12:51.090 --> 00:12:54.180
So you can have a
secure and stable link.

00:12:54.180 --> 00:12:57.930
But what enterprises are looking
for are usually an easier way.

00:12:57.930 --> 00:13:01.350
So I'm happy to announce
that we will soon

00:13:01.350 --> 00:13:04.420
be offering Google Cloud
Interconnect, which provides

00:13:04.420 --> 00:13:07.380
a private access of
private IP addresses

00:13:07.380 --> 00:13:10.560
directly into your project
from your enterprise.

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:13.710
Which enables an easy
hybrid cloud deployment.

00:13:13.710 --> 00:13:15.870
And you don't need
a VPN anymore.

00:13:15.870 --> 00:13:17.400
And we offer this
in two flavors.

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:20.490
Private interconnect
or partner connect.

00:13:20.490 --> 00:13:22.530
Now let's look at
this in detail.

00:13:22.530 --> 00:13:25.770
Private interconnect
means, you met us

00:13:25.770 --> 00:13:29.070
at one of our core location
facilities around the globe.

00:13:29.070 --> 00:13:31.120
And you put your
peering router there,

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:33.090
and you interconnect with us.

00:13:33.090 --> 00:13:35.275
And we drop you directly
into your project.

00:13:37.910 --> 00:13:39.830
Now partner
interconnect means, you

00:13:39.830 --> 00:13:43.160
have a service provider
partner in-between,

00:13:43.160 --> 00:13:45.650
which might be already
in our facility.

00:13:45.650 --> 00:13:47.920
So the service
provider can meet you

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:51.080
where you are located, like at
your enterprise headquarters,

00:13:51.080 --> 00:13:53.300
or your existing data center.

00:13:53.300 --> 00:13:55.790
And depending on the
service, you might not even

00:13:55.790 --> 00:13:56.840
need a router in place.

00:13:56.840 --> 00:13:58.820
But you just get an
ethernet connection which

00:13:58.820 --> 00:14:00.015
drops you into your project.

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:07.220
Now we offer a
private interconnect

00:14:07.220 --> 00:14:09.880
at launch in all the green
locations around the globe.

00:14:09.880 --> 00:14:13.030
So in Europe, that's London,
Amsterdam, Frankfurt.

00:14:13.030 --> 00:14:16.790
But later this year, we
will expand this further

00:14:16.790 --> 00:14:18.640
to more locations,
including Madrid.

00:14:22.580 --> 00:14:26.030
Now once you have a private
interconnect like that,

00:14:26.030 --> 00:14:28.442
you want to also have
this in a reliable way.

00:14:28.442 --> 00:14:29.900
Unfortunately, on
a single circuit,

00:14:29.900 --> 00:14:33.380
we cannot offer you any type
of SLA because just the fiber

00:14:33.380 --> 00:14:34.610
might be cut.

00:14:34.610 --> 00:14:37.150
Someone might trip
over the cable.

00:14:37.150 --> 00:14:38.060
This is not possible.

00:14:38.060 --> 00:14:40.950
But as soon as you have two
lengths in one location,

00:14:40.950 --> 00:14:46.910
we can offer you a 99.9%
availability SLA in that.

00:14:46.910 --> 00:14:51.740
If you go globally, and you have
several interconnections to us

00:14:51.740 --> 00:14:57.560
cross sides, we can offer you
up 5/9 of availability for this.

00:15:00.620 --> 00:15:02.000
Now we talked about scale.

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:03.380
We talked about security.

00:15:03.380 --> 00:15:08.760
And we talked about how you can
interconnect with our network.

00:15:08.760 --> 00:15:12.470
Now I want to take a few
minutes to talk and show you

00:15:12.470 --> 00:15:16.100
how we evaluated the concept
of virtual private cloud, which

00:15:16.100 --> 00:15:18.260
came up in the
industry, and what

00:15:18.260 --> 00:15:20.510
we thought about how we
could make this a little bit

00:15:20.510 --> 00:15:23.620
different.

00:15:23.620 --> 00:15:26.770
Now, if you think about
a virtual private cloud,

00:15:26.770 --> 00:15:29.140
there's a few things
which sets us apart.

00:15:29.140 --> 00:15:30.580
One, that it's global.

00:15:30.580 --> 00:15:33.790
So you can have a single
virtual private cloud

00:15:33.790 --> 00:15:38.090
which spans across many
regions, even across the globe.

00:15:38.090 --> 00:15:39.100
It's shareable.

00:15:39.100 --> 00:15:42.540
And I will go into detail and
show you what that means later.

00:15:42.540 --> 00:15:43.840
It's expandable.

00:15:43.840 --> 00:15:46.870
That means you don't have to
set your network addressing

00:15:46.870 --> 00:15:47.410
in stone.

00:15:47.410 --> 00:15:48.910
You can easily change it later.

00:15:48.910 --> 00:15:51.040
And I will also demo that.

00:15:51.040 --> 00:15:52.720
And you can have
a private access

00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:56.280
to other Google services.

00:15:56.280 --> 00:15:59.750
Now let's go a bit into
detail into all of that.

00:15:59.750 --> 00:16:01.940
Now when I talk
about a global VPC,

00:16:01.940 --> 00:16:04.610
we need to look at a
traditional VPC first.

00:16:04.610 --> 00:16:07.760
Traditionally, virtual
private clouds are regional.

00:16:07.760 --> 00:16:09.830
So once you expand your Google--

00:16:09.830 --> 00:16:13.100
your cloud usage
to another region,

00:16:13.100 --> 00:16:15.020
you might have to set
up an interconnection

00:16:15.020 --> 00:16:17.200
between these regions,
either over the internet,

00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:19.550
over the VPN, or
via other means.

00:16:19.550 --> 00:16:21.240
It's not automatic.

00:16:21.240 --> 00:16:24.080
Now with Google Cloud, this
has always been automatic.

00:16:24.080 --> 00:16:28.820
You can easily
access your machines,

00:16:28.820 --> 00:16:30.590
your virtual machines,
which are located

00:16:30.590 --> 00:16:32.990
in another region
around the globe.

00:16:32.990 --> 00:16:36.710
You can-- there's no extra--

00:16:36.710 --> 00:16:39.560
there's no extra configuration
required for that.

00:16:39.560 --> 00:16:41.630
And the traffic
all flows securely

00:16:41.630 --> 00:16:43.670
through our Google
owned backbone network.

00:16:46.310 --> 00:16:51.230
Now the big difference
between or VPC

00:16:51.230 --> 00:16:53.350
and some other
virtual private cloud

00:16:53.350 --> 00:16:57.500
is also that our VPC can be
shared across an organization.

00:16:57.500 --> 00:17:00.350
Now, when you look
at other VPCs,

00:17:00.350 --> 00:17:04.550
it's kind of, for developers,
very hard to use the cloud.

00:17:04.550 --> 00:17:06.614
Because they might not be
networking specialists.

00:17:06.614 --> 00:17:08.405
They might not know
how to use the network.

00:17:08.405 --> 00:17:11.239
They're actually coming from
an on premise environment,

00:17:11.239 --> 00:17:13.190
and they never had to
care about the network.

00:17:13.190 --> 00:17:15.390
So why do they
suddenly have to set up

00:17:15.390 --> 00:17:20.350
firewall rules or routing rules
or interconnection VPNs, et

00:17:20.350 --> 00:17:21.950
cetera?

00:17:21.950 --> 00:17:23.930
Now luckily, since
in Google, we offer

00:17:23.930 --> 00:17:27.079
shared VPC, or
cross-product network,

00:17:27.079 --> 00:17:29.960
you can have one
virtual network which

00:17:29.960 --> 00:17:32.330
spans across your
whole organization

00:17:32.330 --> 00:17:35.000
and where you provide
the network as a service

00:17:35.000 --> 00:17:37.370
to your developers,
which might still

00:17:37.370 --> 00:17:40.670
be in separate projects for
administration and billing

00:17:40.670 --> 00:17:42.200
purposes.

00:17:42.200 --> 00:17:45.500
Now let's look at
this in a detail.

00:17:45.500 --> 00:17:48.980
You have an organization
where all your projects sit.

00:17:48.980 --> 00:17:50.870
And in this
organization, you create

00:17:50.870 --> 00:17:56.230
a project which will be the
shared VPC host network.

00:17:56.230 --> 00:17:58.610
And that one has the
security and a network admin,

00:17:58.610 --> 00:18:00.570
which care for all
the firewall rules,

00:18:00.570 --> 00:18:04.100
the routing rules, and the
secure interconnection,

00:18:04.100 --> 00:18:06.500
back to your premises.

00:18:06.500 --> 00:18:10.370
Then in this shared network,
you can create subnets.

00:18:10.370 --> 00:18:14.680
And you can share those subnets
across different projects.

00:18:14.680 --> 00:18:16.370
So you can add projects to this.

00:18:16.370 --> 00:18:19.430
Let's say you have different
groups working on rep service,

00:18:19.430 --> 00:18:21.740
recommendations and analytics.

00:18:21.740 --> 00:18:24.600
And all of those need
to talk to each other.

00:18:24.600 --> 00:18:30.260
But centrally, it's decided
who creates those rules.

00:18:30.260 --> 00:18:33.730
So the for the product member,
that's also an advantage.

00:18:33.730 --> 00:18:36.310
Because they get a network
as a plug and play.

00:18:36.310 --> 00:18:37.910
They need to care--
don't need to care

00:18:37.910 --> 00:18:39.457
about the metric security.

00:18:39.457 --> 00:18:41.540
They don't need to care
about a private and secure

00:18:41.540 --> 00:18:44.970
interconnection.

00:18:44.970 --> 00:18:47.610
Now sometimes, you need to
interconnect to other networks

00:18:47.610 --> 00:18:50.910
in Google Cloud, which might
not be in your organization.

00:18:50.910 --> 00:18:52.920
Or you want to do
it the other way,

00:18:52.920 --> 00:18:56.010
where actually, the
project owner wants

00:18:56.010 --> 00:18:57.580
to set the networking rules.

00:18:57.580 --> 00:19:01.830
So for those people, we also
offer a more traditional VPC

00:19:01.830 --> 00:19:05.040
network peering,
where one project says

00:19:05.040 --> 00:19:07.800
he wants to talk to
Project B. Project B

00:19:07.800 --> 00:19:10.570
says he wants to
talk to Project A.

00:19:10.570 --> 00:19:14.370
And we put a secure interconnect
over a software defined network

00:19:14.370 --> 00:19:17.220
in place that you can
talk to each other

00:19:17.220 --> 00:19:20.750
privately without going
through public IP addresses.

00:19:20.750 --> 00:19:22.370
There's even a third way.

00:19:22.370 --> 00:19:25.790
And that is that you can
have now this multi neck.

00:19:25.790 --> 00:19:27.850
You can have one VM.

00:19:27.850 --> 00:19:30.650
Have multiple network
interface cards.

00:19:30.650 --> 00:19:33.360
And those can be in
different networks.

00:19:33.360 --> 00:19:36.440
So, this is, for
example, used in many

00:19:36.440 --> 00:19:41.000
of the virtual appliances
I talked about earlier.

00:19:41.000 --> 00:19:43.700
Last but not least, I want to
talk about the private access

00:19:43.700 --> 00:19:45.330
to Google services.

00:19:45.330 --> 00:19:48.190
Now, often enough,
you want to run

00:19:48.190 --> 00:19:52.190
some back end calculations,
or some micro services,

00:19:52.190 --> 00:19:52.940
which don't need--

00:19:52.940 --> 00:19:54.610
shouldn't talk to
the outside world.

00:19:54.610 --> 00:19:56.270
They shouldn't have a public IP.

00:19:56.270 --> 00:19:58.760
But you still want to make use
of all the Google services.

00:19:58.760 --> 00:20:00.830
Just cloud storage,
cloud logging,

00:20:00.830 --> 00:20:03.350
BigQuery, or any of the others.

00:20:03.350 --> 00:20:05.600
So, this was very
difficult in the past.

00:20:05.600 --> 00:20:08.750
Because you talk over
public IPs to those.

00:20:08.750 --> 00:20:11.010
With private access
to Google services,

00:20:11.010 --> 00:20:14.780
you can enable that although
these virtual machines

00:20:14.780 --> 00:20:16.370
don't have a private IP--

00:20:16.370 --> 00:20:18.410
don't have a public
IP, they can still

00:20:18.410 --> 00:20:20.990
talk to other resources
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:20:23.960 --> 00:20:28.070
Now I want to take that a little
bit into practice and demo

00:20:28.070 --> 00:20:29.330
this.

00:20:29.330 --> 00:20:32.750
So let me show you a
little scenario we have.

00:20:32.750 --> 00:20:34.850
Let's say we have
this scenario where

00:20:34.850 --> 00:20:37.520
you have three teams, one
working on the web server,

00:20:37.520 --> 00:20:39.500
one working on the
recommendation engine,

00:20:39.500 --> 00:20:43.370
and one working on
the analytics, who

00:20:43.370 --> 00:20:45.105
use a lot of BigQuery.

00:20:45.105 --> 00:20:48.170
Now in a traditional
VPC, you would put all

00:20:48.170 --> 00:20:50.180
of those in different projects.

00:20:50.180 --> 00:20:51.860
And then, to talk
to each other, you

00:20:51.860 --> 00:20:53.690
need to put
interconnects into place

00:20:53.690 --> 00:20:55.490
between all these projects.

00:20:55.490 --> 00:20:58.370
You need to put
connections into place

00:20:58.370 --> 00:21:02.019
from each of those projects
back to your on premise network.

00:21:02.019 --> 00:21:03.560
And you somehow need
to make sure you

00:21:03.560 --> 00:21:07.250
can talk to our other services.

00:21:07.250 --> 00:21:09.350
Now with the features
I just explained,

00:21:09.350 --> 00:21:10.830
let's take a step back.

00:21:10.830 --> 00:21:13.550
What you actually
want to achieve.

00:21:13.550 --> 00:21:16.430
You just want to talk
securely between the projects,

00:21:16.430 --> 00:21:18.500
securely back home.

00:21:18.500 --> 00:21:23.040
And you want to talk securely
to other Google Cloud Services.

00:21:23.040 --> 00:21:24.950
How you do that
with Google Cloud

00:21:24.950 --> 00:21:31.222
with VPC's is you still
have the different teams

00:21:31.222 --> 00:21:32.180
and different projects.

00:21:32.180 --> 00:21:36.580
They get their separate
bills split out.

00:21:36.580 --> 00:21:38.590
You can set ident access--

00:21:38.590 --> 00:21:40.610
access to those separately.

00:21:40.610 --> 00:21:43.150
But you have one shared
virtual private cloud,

00:21:43.150 --> 00:21:46.570
one cross project networking
spanning all of those projects.

00:21:46.570 --> 00:21:51.130
You have a secure
connection to Cloud BigQuery

00:21:51.130 --> 00:21:52.990
over private access.

00:21:52.990 --> 00:21:56.500
And you also have a private
interconnect in place

00:21:56.500 --> 00:21:58.000
back to your on premise network.

00:21:58.000 --> 00:21:59.290
Only one.

00:21:59.290 --> 00:22:01.330
And the developers
just have to care

00:22:01.330 --> 00:22:05.720
about setting up their machines
and setting up their service.

00:22:05.720 --> 00:22:09.961
Now let's show you how this
looks like in our environment.

00:22:14.200 --> 00:22:16.600
So this is our Google
Cloud Platform dashboard.

00:22:16.600 --> 00:22:18.580
And this is the networking page.

00:22:18.580 --> 00:22:21.460
So, there's actually
two kinds of networks.

00:22:21.460 --> 00:22:22.900
There's automatic
networks, where

00:22:22.900 --> 00:22:25.330
we set up some sub
networks for you

00:22:25.330 --> 00:22:29.290
around the globe, one
in each of our regions.

00:22:29.290 --> 00:22:33.130
And then there's the
second way, which

00:22:33.130 --> 00:22:36.670
is custom networking,
which you see on the bottom

00:22:36.670 --> 00:22:40.100
where we set up--

00:22:40.100 --> 00:22:44.040
where you set up the networking
and the addressing yourself.

00:22:44.040 --> 00:22:45.710
Now, let me show
you how easy you

00:22:45.710 --> 00:22:49.860
can add a sub network to this.

00:22:49.860 --> 00:22:51.030
So I go into the network.

00:22:51.030 --> 00:22:52.610
I say I want to add a subnet.

00:22:52.610 --> 00:22:54.319
Let's say our
developers came and say

00:22:54.319 --> 00:22:55.610
they want the test environment.

00:22:55.610 --> 00:22:58.460
So I call the subnet
network Def test.

00:22:58.460 --> 00:23:00.320
I need to say in
which region it is.

00:23:00.320 --> 00:23:02.120
Let's say Europe.

00:23:02.120 --> 00:23:04.310
And I need to give it
an IP address range.

00:23:04.310 --> 00:23:07.410
I give it 100000/24.

00:23:07.410 --> 00:23:08.680
I hit Add.

00:23:08.680 --> 00:23:11.470
And I added a new sub network.

00:23:11.470 --> 00:23:14.920
Now I want to give all
the developers access

00:23:14.920 --> 00:23:18.050
to my sub network
over share VPC.

00:23:18.050 --> 00:23:20.680
So I go into share
VPC, and I start--

00:23:23.460 --> 00:23:24.740
still there.

00:23:24.740 --> 00:23:29.430
Let me delete it and show
you something else quickly.

00:23:29.430 --> 00:23:30.370
This is the demo.

00:23:33.190 --> 00:23:35.160
So let me start from scratch.

00:23:35.160 --> 00:23:37.290
This takes about a second.

00:23:37.290 --> 00:23:40.270
Let's say in the meantime,
the developers come back.

00:23:40.270 --> 00:23:42.550
And they say, what did you do?

00:23:42.550 --> 00:23:45.580
You gave us just 200
addresses, just 200 VM--

00:23:45.580 --> 00:23:46.930
250 addresses.

00:23:46.930 --> 00:23:49.240
We need more than
250 VMs for testing.

00:23:49.240 --> 00:23:51.460
Give us a few more.

00:23:51.460 --> 00:23:53.990
So what we will do, we
can just easily say,

00:23:53.990 --> 00:23:54.970
OK, I made a mistake.

00:23:54.970 --> 00:23:57.520
I gave them only a
slash 24 network.

00:23:57.520 --> 00:24:02.410
I go back and I select a
different subnet range.

00:24:02.410 --> 00:24:06.730
And I say I want to extend
this network, not 100000/24,

00:24:06.730 --> 00:24:08.200
but slash 16.

00:24:08.200 --> 00:24:11.110
So now they can use up
to 65,000 addresses.

00:24:11.110 --> 00:24:14.470
I hope that should be enough.

00:24:14.470 --> 00:24:16.960
Now while this is
creating, let me finally

00:24:16.960 --> 00:24:18.400
step through the
Wizard and set up

00:24:18.400 --> 00:24:20.085
the cross-product networking.

00:24:22.820 --> 00:24:26.150
Now first step to enable
cross-product networking, is

00:24:26.150 --> 00:24:29.490
you need to enable a
project as a host project.

00:24:29.490 --> 00:24:31.730
So I want to say, this
is my host project.

00:24:31.730 --> 00:24:34.850
This is the project where
the security firewall network

00:24:34.850 --> 00:24:36.430
administration will happen.

00:24:36.430 --> 00:24:37.400
I do this.

00:24:37.400 --> 00:24:38.600
I save.

00:24:38.600 --> 00:24:41.350
And then the next
step, I need to say,

00:24:41.350 --> 00:24:45.560
which subnets do I want to
share with another environment?

00:24:45.560 --> 00:24:49.280
I can either select them one by
one, analytics for an analytics

00:24:49.280 --> 00:24:50.510
project, etc.

00:24:50.510 --> 00:24:53.450
For purposes of the demo,
I will just share them all.

00:24:53.450 --> 00:24:54.075
And I continue.

00:24:58.730 --> 00:24:59.490
OK?

00:24:59.490 --> 00:25:01.200
And then, the last
step is, I need

00:25:01.200 --> 00:25:04.160
to say with which projects do
I want to share the network?

00:25:04.160 --> 00:25:07.780
So I will use my analytics
recommendation and web server

00:25:07.780 --> 00:25:09.240
project.

00:25:09.240 --> 00:25:10.830
I save this.

00:25:10.830 --> 00:25:14.230
And now the cross project
networking is put into place.

00:25:14.230 --> 00:25:16.580
Now we create a
secure connection

00:25:16.580 --> 00:25:18.780
that from the other
projects you can

00:25:18.780 --> 00:25:22.050
create virtual machines
automatically and easily

00:25:22.050 --> 00:25:22.740
in this project.

00:25:22.740 --> 00:25:25.020
Now how does this look like?

00:25:25.020 --> 00:25:26.520
Let me switch to
another project.

00:25:26.520 --> 00:25:29.370
Let's say I'm a developer
on the analytics team,

00:25:29.370 --> 00:25:32.910
and I want to create
a virtual machine.

00:25:32.910 --> 00:25:35.820
So I go to Compute Engine.

00:25:35.820 --> 00:25:37.262
VM instances.

00:25:37.262 --> 00:25:38.845
I say I want to
create a new instance.

00:25:41.470 --> 00:25:43.080
Let's call it analytics 1.

00:25:49.190 --> 00:25:51.863
I want to have this in Europe.

00:25:51.863 --> 00:25:54.630
Again.

00:25:54.630 --> 00:25:59.920
And, under networking, I can
select now in which network

00:25:59.920 --> 00:26:00.780
this lies.

00:26:00.780 --> 00:26:02.650
It's right now in
the default network.

00:26:02.650 --> 00:26:04.870
And here, I can select I
want to actually put it

00:26:04.870 --> 00:26:07.240
in a network which
was shared with me.

00:26:07.240 --> 00:26:09.670
And I want to select
the analytics network,

00:26:09.670 --> 00:26:13.330
because I'm a member
of the analytics team.

00:26:13.330 --> 00:26:14.860
And that's all I need to do.

00:26:14.860 --> 00:26:18.550
I create-- I create
the VM just normally.

00:26:18.550 --> 00:26:20.800
And then, all the other
teams can access it

00:26:20.800 --> 00:26:24.430
according to the rules which
were centrally defined.

00:26:24.430 --> 00:26:27.300
So this is how easily you
can create a shared network

00:26:27.300 --> 00:26:29.800
and share it across different
teams and different projects.

00:26:36.380 --> 00:26:42.440
Now to recap, we have a
global virtual private cloud,

00:26:42.440 --> 00:26:45.870
with private connectivity and
private access to other Google

00:26:45.870 --> 00:26:46.370
services.

00:26:50.680 --> 00:26:54.380
We have it shareable, so you can
share across different teams.

00:26:54.380 --> 00:26:57.350
And you can use a shared
interconnection to your

00:26:57.350 --> 00:26:59.450
on premise environment.

00:26:59.450 --> 00:27:01.070
And we have it expandable.

00:27:01.070 --> 00:27:04.130
You don't need to do the
whole planning ahead of time.

00:27:04.130 --> 00:27:09.520
You can expand the IP address
ranges at a later stage.

00:27:09.520 --> 00:27:12.590
And that's some
of the advantages

00:27:12.590 --> 00:27:15.404
Google virtual private
cloud provides for us.

00:27:15.404 --> 00:27:16.820
And with this, I
want to give over

00:27:16.820 --> 00:27:19.700
to Raul, who will talk
about BT's approach to build

00:27:19.700 --> 00:27:22.040
your hybrid infrastructure.

00:27:22.040 --> 00:27:23.400
Thank you.

00:27:23.400 --> 00:27:28.570
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:28.570 --> 00:27:30.340
RAUL CHICO: Thank you Jens.

00:27:30.340 --> 00:27:32.980
[SPEAKING SPANISH]

00:44:05.960 --> 00:44:07.810
